**What each page would cover:**

**`freshwater-fish`** — River/lake fish (grass carp, pike, sport fish, sardines). Systematically different Hg and As pathway from marine fish (sediment-driven vs. oceanic bioaccumulation). Sources span Brazil (Tapajós), Canada (Mackenzie), Latvia, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Thailand, Tanzania. Would be a sibling of `fish.md`.

**`products/seafood`** — Consumer-facing seafood product category (retail fish, restaurant fish, canned/fresh/frozen). Note: `ingredients/seafood.md` already exists as a taxonomy placeholder for the raw commodity; the product page is the regulatory/advisory view. FDA/EPA fish consumption advice, EU regulatory limits, MeHg advisories by species type.

**`cereals`** — Broad Codex/EFSA/JECFA grain category (wheat, barley, rye, oats, maize, including rice) used in maximum-level setting. All 8 sources are regulatory/authoritative: Codex standard CXS-193, EFSA cadmium/lead/nickel opinions, JECFA cadmium assessment. Would be the regulatory anchor page; existing `wheat.md`, `oat.md`, `non-rice-grains.md` link from it.

**`shellfish`** — Bivalves + crustaceans (broader than existing `bivalve-molluscs.md`). Crustacean hepatopancreas has a notably different Cd profile. FDA/EPA fish consumption advice uses shellfish as a distinct MeHg advisory matrix. Would be parent to `bivalve-molluscs.md`; future `crustaceans.md` if warranted.

**`breastmilk`** — Human breast milk as an infant exposure matrix. Key comparator in the formula/breastmilk arsenic literature — breast milk is generally very low in iAs vs. rice-based formula or cereal. Thoerig 2025 is a systematic review specifically on toxic elements in human milk vs. formula. Carignan 2015/2016 are the canonical breastfeeding/formula arsenic cohort papers.

**`quinoa`** — Andean grain with distinct Cd pathway: high-altitude Peruvian soils are naturally elevated in Cd, and quinoa concentrates it differently from other grains. All 6 sources specifically measure quinoa (not as a generic grain). Includes the Consumer Reports 2014 grain survey, which found quinoa had significantly lower iAs than rice.

